<title>
it works
</title>
<date>
June 4, 2006
</date>
<reviewer>
Philip Koch
</reviewer>
<reviewer_location>
Maine
</reviewer_location>
<review_text>
We are very pleased with this phone.  We needed a 5.8 GHz system in order to avoid interference with a wireless computer network.  We initially bought an AT&T-branded phone, but it regularly crashed (I'm guessing that is what happened... the handset went dead and then seemed to "re-boot" about 5-10 minutes into a call.)  AT&T customer support had no suggestions other than return the phone, which we did, replacing it with this Panasonic model.

Many phones advertised as 5.8 GHz are in fact only 5.8 from the base station to the handset; they are 2.4 GHz from the handset to the base station.  The AT&T phone mentioned above was "dual frequency", as were most of the others we checked out at the local big box stores.  Only the Panasonics were 5.8 in both directions.  This ought to make a difference, especially with 802.11 (ie, computer network) interference.  This was why we picked the Panasonic.

My only complaint is -- as others have mentioned -- the spurious "missed call" message(s) on one handset after another handset was used to take the call.  Panasonic is not the only brand to feature this annoying UI, unfortunately.

Overall, we recommend this phone.  I'd definitely purchase it again
</review_text>
</review>
